```javascript
function Stack() {
    var items = [];

    // add a new item to the top of the stack
    this.push = function(element) {
        items.push(element);
    }

    // remove an element from the stack
    this.pop = function() {
        if(items.length == 0)
            return "Underflow";
        return items.pop();
    }

    // view the topmost element of the stack
    this.peek = function() {
        return items[items.length - 1];
    }

    // check if the stack is empty
    this.isEmpty = function() {
        return items.length == 0;
    }

    // return the number of elements in the stack
    this.size = function(){
        return items.length;
    }

    // print the stack
    this.print = function(){
        console.log(items.toString());
    }
}
```
For JavaScript, it's important to note that popping an element off an empty array will return `undefined`, not throw an error like it would in other languages. It's explicitly stated to return "Underflow" when trying to pop an item from an empty stack. The rest of the methods mimic typical behavior of a stack: `push` and `pop` change the stack, while `peek` and `isEmpty` allow you to check its current state.

The `size` method returns the number of items currently in the stack. 

The `print` method logs the current stack to the console. It's a useful debugging tool.

The `Stack` function creates a new stack instance. Those methods are all directly available on any stack instance created using this construction.

Please note that this code does not handle both types of errors and null values. You may need additional error handling for strict mode applications.

For example, if you want to use the stack object then:
```javascript
var stack = new Stack();
stack.push(10); // It will add 10 to the top of the stack
stack.push(20); // It will add 20 to the top of the stack
stack.push(30); // It will add 30 to the top of the stack
stack.print();  // It will print the stack [10, 20, 30]
```
The `size` method can be used to find out the size of the stack : 
```javascript
console.log(stack.size()); // It will print 3 which denotes the size of the stack
```
The `isEmpty` method returns a boolean which denotes whether the stack is empty or not : 
```javascript
console.log(stack.isEmpty()); // It will print false
```
The `pop` method removes the topmost element from the stack: 
```javascript
console.log(stack.pop()); // It will remove the topmost element 30 from the stack and print it
```
The `peek` method will look at the topmost element without removing it : 
```javascript
console.log(stack.peek()); // It will print 20 which denotes the topmost element of the stack
```
Note: Always verify the size of stack before peek or pop operation as these methods can throw an error if the stack is empty.
Note: The pop operation will return 'Underflow' if you try to execute it on an empty stack.
Note: The push operation will add an element to the stack.
Note: The peek operation will return the topmost element of the stack.
Note: The isEmpty operation will return true if the stack is empty and false otherwise.
Note: The size operation will return the count of elements in the stack.
Note: The print operation will print all the elements of the stack.

